      "text": "To find the altitude of a triangle when the area and the length of the base are known, we can use the formula for the area of a triangle:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{altitude} \\]\n\nWe are given:\n- The area of the triangle is 600 square feet.\n- The length of the base is 30 feet.\n\nWe need to find the altitude. Let's denote the altitude by \\( h \\). Substituting the given values into the area formula, we get:\n\n\\[ 600 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 30 \\times h \\]\n\nTo isolate \\( h \\), we first simplify the right side of the equation:\n\n\\[ 600 = 15h \\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\( h \\) by dividing both sides of the equation by 15:\n\n\\[ h = \\frac{600}{15} \\]\n\n\\[ h = 40 \\]\n\nTherefore, the altitude of the triangle is \\(\\boxed{40}\\) feet.",
